{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.2 and 9 distinguish documentation from reliability evidence. Task and scoring descriptions support transparency, but one score cannot establish repeatability or instability. Repeated AI evaluations reinterpret test-retest assessment.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations are undocumented; whether they occurred is unknown."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "One score supplies no evidence of variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9, the execution record establishes that repeatability was not studied. This absence of testing does not demonstrate poor reliability or unstable scores.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The record explicitly rules out the claimed repeated-evaluation study."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "Without repeated observations, instability is undetermined."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7 guide assessment of repeated AI evaluations. The observed range is two percentage points and meets the stated three-point rule. This narrow finding does not establish broader reliability or justify an EFPA coefficient rating.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The same AI and problems were evaluated repeatedly under identical stated conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The two-point range is within the permitted three-point maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7 support examining repeated-score variation. The observed 31-point range fails the report's stability rule, providing adverse evidence for this comparison without establishing reliability in other settings.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluations used the same AI, problems and stated conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The 31-point range exceeds the stated three-point maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1, 6.2 and 9 separate item quality, documentation and reliability. Topic and scoring descriptions establish neither repeated-evaluation evidence nor answer errors; both remain unassessed from this material.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The material does not establish whether identical-condition repeats were studied."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "No answer errors are demonstrated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_review": "The appendix adds technical guidance relevant to EFPA §6.3.8. Under §§6.1 and 9, installation information supplies no additional evidence of answer quality or repeatability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results",
          "reason": "The appendix does not resolve the missing execution history."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no further evaluation results or examination of answers",
          "reason": "Technical instructions do not demonstrate mistakes in supplied answers."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.1 distinguishes content-review procedures from their results. Independent examination is documented, but released-answer accuracy is unknown. Reconciliation supplies no pre-reconciliation agreement estimate under §9.6.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "Both questions and answers underwent development review."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "Review procedures alone do not establish that every released answer is correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1 and 6.3.2 distinguish documented review from correct scoring materials. Review occurred, yet twelve released answers are explicitly wrong. Editor agreement remains unquantified under §9.6.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "The development review covered both questions and supplied answers."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "The identified errors directly refute universal answer correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.2 requires evidence that automated scoring operates correctly. Deterministic marking establishes consistency for identical text, not correctness. Under §9.6, inter-rater reliability is inapplicable to the described fixed, nonjudgmental scoring rule.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported.",
          "reason": "Such testing is unreported, rather than explicitly ruled out."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "Consistent marks need not be correct marks."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.2 identifies direct scoring verification evidence: twenty responses were checked and two discrepancies remain. Determinism does not resolve these correctness failures. Fixed-rule scoring does not require inter-rater reliability under §9.6.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly documents comparison against independent correct marks."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved.",
          "reason": "Observed disagreements with established correct marks refute correctness for every possible response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10 evaluates validity for the stated interpretation. The observed counts suffice for this narrowly descriptive comparison. No general intelligence inference is needed, and the raw-score comparison requires no normative transformation.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported solution counts directly answer the stated question."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "A solved more problems in the reported evaluation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §§10.1–10.5, validity depends on the intended inference. The grid results support the local comparison but do not establish general intelligence across subjects and settings; broader content and generalisation evidence are missing.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not establish sufficiency for the stated general intelligence inference."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "The observed counts establish A's advantage on these evaluated problems."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3.2, 6.3.2 and 10 assess scoring against its intended interpretation. Binary credit matches the explicit complete-grid requirement. Lack of partial credit is not itself a defect for this purpose.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "The one-or-zero scoring rule implements that requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Zero credit for partially correct grids follows the stated requirement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§3.2, 6.3.2 and 10 identify an explicit mismatch between implemented scoring and the intended interpretation. Whole-grid binary credit cannot implement the stated requirement to credit each correct cell.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "give a response credit for every correctly completed cell",
          "reason": "The implemented rule gives no credit to partially correct grids."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "including partially correct output grids",
          "reason": "The requirement explicitly includes credit that the rule withholds."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3, 9.4 and 9.7 distinguish identical-condition repeatability from variation across presentations. Instruction wording is deliberately varied, so this studies instruction sensitivity rather than identical-condition repeatability. Formal alternate-form equivalence is not established.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No evaluation repeats an identical instruction.",
          "reason": "Instructions differ, so the evaluation conditions are not identical."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "Scores are compared while instruction wording changes and the other stated conditions remain fixed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7 apply to repeated AI evaluations under identical stated conditions. Instruction sensitivity is not studied. Without numerical results, the magnitude or adequacy of repeatability cannot be judged.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "software, resources, problem order, settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes across evaluations with identical stated conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied.",
          "reason": "The study explicitly excludes instruction changes."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task instruction required clarification. The complete EFPA 2025 framework informed scope: Part A description and scoring; Part B documentation, materials, norms, reliability, validity, fair use, reports and final evaluation; Part C source attribution. Case reviews focus on relevant provisions because full questionnaires were expressly unnecessary. AI systems replace human test takers, executions replace testing occasions, and instruction variants are treated as presentation variants. Human identity checks, sensory or motor accommodations, clinical feedback and human qualification categories are inapplicable as literal questions about AI examinees; benchmark-user guidance and fairness of comparisons remain relevant where evidenced. Unreported norms, materials, subgroup analyses or generated-report properties are unassessed, not presumed inadequate or inapplicable. No numerical EFPA ratings or aggregate quality score were assigned; the required claim labels are separate from EFPA ratings. Only supplied material was used. Framework attribution: EFPA (2025), Test Review Model, version 5.0, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0. This application does not imply EFPA endorsement."
}
